ible exit codes. Writing the test...\n\nActually, I'm realizing there's a logical issue here — if the read end of the pipe closes and chronic tries to write, it'll get SIGPIPE regardless of whether a handler is installed, so the test should work as intended without hanging.\n\nBut wait, I need to think through what the SIGPIPE handler actually does. Without it, Go ignores SIGPIPE and the write just returns an error. With it, the program exits cleanly. Either way, chronic continues and exits normally — it just returns the subprocess's exit code. So the handler doesn't really change chronic's behavior in any meaningful way for this scenario.\n\nFor a streaming tool like `cat`, SIGPIPE handling matters because it prevents wasted work — without it, the tool keeps trying to write to a closed pipe. But chronic buffers all output first, so a failed write just means it exits with the subprocess code. The SIGPIPE handler would change the exit code to 0, but both outcomes are graceful.\n\nFor the R2.3 test, I should focus on verifying that chronic doesn't crash or hang when the pipe breaks — the exact exit code difference is less critical.\n\nWriting the test...\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the TestCommandNotFound test — I don't actually need to set LC_ALL=C since there's no output comparison happening, so I can strip that out and keep it simpler. Writing the test file...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":612,"cache_read_input_tokens":44535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":612},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11219621-a4aa-469f-8167-d9d655f0c002","uuid":"9f8c8f2a-4934-465e-98b1-7e92d13cac0a"}
